LLM_output:
Across Billboard charts, music critics, and fanbase lists, these 10 songs stand out as Fall Out Boy's most influential and iconic tracks:

### 1. "Sugar, We're Goin Down" (*From Under the Cork Tree*, 2005)  &#x20;

The ultimate pop-punk anthem that launched Fall Out Boy into mainstream superstardom. Driven by Patrick Stump's rapid-fire vocal delivery and Pete Wentz's iconic lyrics, it became a defining track of the mid-2000s alt-rock boom.

### 2. "Dance, Dance" (*From Under the Cork Tree*, 2005)  &#x20;

Built around Andy Hurley's driving drums and a classic bassline, this track earned the band a Grammy nomination and became a dance-floor staple in rock clubs worldwide.

### 3. "Thnks fr th Mmrs" (*Infinity on High*, 2007)  &#x20;

An orchestral-meets-pop-punk blend featuring a famous string section and a memorable music video starring Kim Kardashian. It stands as one of the band's biggest commercial crossover hits.  &#x20;

### 4. "This Ain't a Scene, It's an Arms Race" (*Infinity on High*, 2007)  &#x20;

The lead single from *Infinity on High* peaked at #2 on the Billboard Hot 100. With its R&B-influenced vocal rhythms and massive guitar riffs, it served as a tongue-in-cheek commentary on scene culture and fame.

### 5. "Grand Theft Autumn / Where Is Your Boy" (*Take This to Your Grave*, 2003)  &#x20;

The definitive early Fall Out Boy track. This breakout single from their debut album established the dual dynamic of Stump's soulful vocals and Wentz's witty lyrics that defined their early underground popularity.  &#x20;

### 6. "Centuries" (*American Beauty / American Psycho*, 2015)  &#x20;

Sampling Suzanne Vega's "Tom's Diner," this arena-rock anthem became a multi-platinum stadium staple and one of the highest-charting rock tracks of the 2010s.

### 7. "My Songs Know What You Did in the Dark (Light Em Up)" (*Save Rock and Roll*, 2013)  &#x20;

The lead single from their 2013 comeback album after a four-year hiatus. The track transformed their sound into a heavy, foot-stomping pop-rock style.

### 8. "A Little Less Sixteen Candles, a Little More 'Touch Me'" (*From Under the Cork Tree*, 2005)  &#x20;

A classic example of the band's mid-2000s style, featuring clever song titles, heavy guitar work, and a vampire-themed music video.

### 9. "What a Catch, Donnie" (*Folie à Deux*, 2008)  &#x20;

An emotional piano ballad from *Folie à Deux*. The song features guest vocals from alternative icons like Elvis Costello, Alexander DeLeon, and Brendon Urie singing refrains of previous Fall Out Boy hits during the climax.  &#x20;

### 10. "Disloyal Order of Water Buffaloes" (*Folie à Deux*, 2008)  &#x20;

Although not a major radio single, this album opener is widely regarded by longtime fans and critics as one of the band's best arrangements, blending organs, soaring vocals, and introspective lyrics.

**Honorable Mentions:**

- "Uma Thurman" (*American Beauty / American Psycho*, 2015)  &#x20;
- "Saturday" (*Take This to Your Grave*, 2003)  &#x20;
- "Love From the Other Side" (*So Much (for) Stardust*, 2023)

